Recommends: flatpak, snapd, systemd
Description: Free up disk space on Ubuntu, safely
 Ubuntu quietly fills up with cache, build output and package archives you
 never asked for. Purge finds it, marks what is safe to remove, and clears
 it in one click.
 .
 Every item carries a plain-English explanation and a safety label. Deletion
 is allowlist-only: anything the app cannot identify is never shown, let
 alone removed. Personal files are moved to the Trash so they can be
 restored; caches are removed outright, because trashing them would not free
 any space.
 .
 It scans app caches (deb, snap and flatpak browsers included), developer
 caches for npm, pip, cargo, Go, Gradle, Maven, Flutter and Dart, per-project
 build output, large personal files and duplicates, and Ubuntu system storage
 such as APT archives, old kernels, journald logs and old snap revisions.
